.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Libacme
is plan9port's library for programs that use
.IR acme (1)
windows.
It has plan9port's interface, so that a program written for it compiles here,
but it opens, reads and writes the window's files in
.B /mnt/acme
(see
.IR acme (4))
instead of using a 9P client library.
.PP
When
.B /mnt/amux
has the window, the library opens the window's
.B clone
there and uses the session's directory in place of acme's
(see
.IR amux (4)),
so the window's address, limit and guard are the program's own.
It keeps the
.B clone
file open while it keeps the window's files, so the session lasts that long.
Otherwise it opens acme's files directly.
.PP
A
.B Win
is a window and the files of it the library has opened.
A program should not use its fields, but for
.BR id ,
the window's number.
.I Newwin
makes a window, by acme's
.BR new/ctl ,
and returns its
.BR Win .
.I Openwin
returns a
.B Win
for the existing window
.IR id .
Both return nil, with the error string set, if they cannot open the window,
as when acme deletes it before they open it.
If
.I ctlfd
is not negative, it is the window's
.B ctl
file in acme, open for reading and writing, and the library keeps it;
with a session, the library writes
.B lock
and
.B unlock
to it.
.PP
The library opens a window's files as they are first used and keeps them open,
with
.B OCEXEC
set, until
.I winclosefiles
or
.I winfree
closes them.
It opens each file once, under a lock, so procs that share a
.B Win
may use its files first in any order.
Closing them ends the window's session, if it has one,
unless something else still holds a file of it:
a file descriptor from
.IR winfd ,
.I winopenfd
or
.IR winseqopen ,
a bind of the directory
.I winpath
gives, or a command
.I pipetowin
or
.I pipewinto
runs.
A later use makes a new session, whose
.B addr
starts at
.BR #0,#0 ,
as
.IR amux (4)
says.
.I Winfree
also frees the
.BR Win .
.I Windeleteall
writes
.B delete
to the
.B ctl
file of every window the program has.
.PP
Many of these functions take a format
.I fmt
and its arguments, as
.IR print (2)
does;
.I fmt\fR, \fP...
below is the formatted text.
.PP
.I Winaddr
writes
.I fmt\fR, \fP...
to the window's
.B addr
file,
and
.I winreadaddr
reads it back: it returns the address's first rune offset and puts the second in
.BI * q1\fR.
.I Winctl
writes
.I fmt\fR, \fP...
to the window's
.B ctl
file.
It takes any message of
.IR acme (4),
including the ones for a body a program draws
.RB ( draw ,
.BR menu ,
.BR nomenu ,
.BR undo
and
.BR redo ).
A session refuses
.B lock
and
.BR unlock ,
so the lines of a write that start with either go to acme's own
.B ctl
for the window, in their order among the others,
and the library keeps that file open while it keeps the window's files.
A write that fails partway leaves the messages before the failure done.
.I Winname
sets the window's name to
.I fmt\fR, \fP...
by its
.B ctl
file.
.I Windel
deletes the window, writing
.B del
to
.BR ctl ,
or
.B delete
if
.I sure
is set.
.PP
.I Winprint
writes
.I fmt\fR, \fP...
to the window's
.IR file ,
and
.I winwrite
writes the
.I n
bytes at
.IR a .
.I Winread
reads at most
.I n
bytes of
.I file
into
.IR a ,
and
.I winmread
reads the rest of
.I file
into a buffer it allocates, which the caller frees.
.I Winseek
seeks the window's open
.IR file ,
as
.IR seek (2)
does.
The
.I file
these take is one of
.BR addr ,
.BR body ,
.BR ctl ,
.BR data ,
.BR event ,
.BR rdevent ,
.B tag
and
.BR xdata .
.I Winfd
and
.I winopenfd
open any of the window's files for
.I mode
and return the file descriptor, which the caller closes.
.I Winpath
returns the directory the library opens them in, the session's or acme's,
for a program that binds them, as one that draws its body binds its
.B mouse
and
.B cons
on
.BR /dev ;
the string is the library's, and good until
.I winclosefiles
or
.IR winfree .
.PP
An
.B Event
is one of the window's events, as
.IR acme (4)
describes them:
.TF oq0,oq1
.TP
.BR c1 ", " c2
The origin and the kind of the event.
.TP
.BR q0 ", " q1
Where it happened, in runes.
When the event is a click (an empty
.BR q0 ,
.BR q1 )
with an expansion (the 2 bit in
.BR flag ),
they are the expansion's, and so are
.BR text ,
.B nr
and
.BR nb .
.TP
.BR oq0 ", " oq1
The event's own
.B q0
and
.BR q1 ,
even when acme expanded it.
.TP
.B flag
The event's flag.
.TP
.B nr
The number of runes in
.BR text .
.TP
.B nb
The number of bytes in
.BR text .
.TP
.B text
The event's text, in UTF, or empty if it is too long for acme to send.
.TP
.B arg
The chorded argument, when the 8 bit is set in
.BR flag ;
otherwise empty.
.TP
.B loc
Where the argument came from, as a button 3 address with the file name;
otherwise empty.
.PD
.PP
.I Winreadevent
reads the next event from the window's
.B event
file, with the messages that follow it for an expansion and for an argument.
It returns 1, 0 at the end of the file, and \-1 with the error string set if the message is not an event.
.I Winwriteevent
writes the event back to
.BR event ,
for
.I acme
to do what it would have done had the file not been open.
It writes
.B q0
and
.BR q1 ,
so an expanded event goes back as its expansion.
.I Eventfmt
formats an
.B Event*
for a verb installed with
.I fmtinstall
(see
.IR fmtinstall (2));
it uses
.BR %q ,
which needs
.IR quotefmtinstall .
.PP
.I Wineventchan
returns a channel of
.BR Event* .
Its first call makes the channel and a proc that calls
.I winreadevent
and sends each event; nil comes after the last.
The proc fills two
.BR Event s
in turn, so an event stays good until the program receives the one after the next.
A program that uses
.I wineventchan
should not call
.IR winreadevent .
.PP
.I Winreadrdevent
reads the window's
.B rdevent
file as
.I winreadevent
reads
.BR event ,
with the expansion and the argument that follow an event.
Its
.B S
events, with
.B c2
.BR S ,
say that the body's selection is
.B q0
to
.BR q1 .
.I Winopenrdevent
opens
.B rdevent
again, closing it if it is open; when
.I seq
is not negative, the file starts after the body's edit
.IR seq ,
and its first read fails with
.L "seq mismatch"
if that edit is no longer kept.
Without it,
.I winreadrdevent
opens the file at its first call, from then on.
.PP
.I Winseq
reads the window's
.B seq
file: the number of edits made to the body, or \-1 on error.
.I Winseqopen
writes
.I seq
to the window's
.B seq
file and opens its
.I file
for
.IR mode ,
so that a read or write through the file descriptor it returns
fails with
.L "seq mismatch"
if an edit to the body has come after edit
.IR seq .
The caller closes it.
.I Winseqwrite
writes
.I addr
to the window's
.B addr
file, if it is not nil, and then, through
.B data
opened as
.I winseqopen
opens it, the
.I n
bytes at
.IR a ,
which replace the address's text, or delete it when
.I n
is 0.
It writes them in pieces of whole runes, one
.B Undo
for them all.
It returns
.IR n ,
or \-1 if an edit to the body came after
.IR seq .
The library writes
.B seq
and opens the file it guards under one lock, which its own opens of
.BR body ,
.BR data ,
.B xdata
and
.B rdevent
also take, so that none of the program's opens takes another's guard.
.PP
.I Pipetowin
runs the
.IR rc (1)
command
.I fmt\fR, \fP...
with
.B /dev/null
as its standard input and the window's
.I file
as its standard output.
Its standard error is
.I fderr
if that is not zero, and otherwise
.I file
too.
.I Pipewinto
runs it with the window's
.I file
as its standard input and
.I fdout
as its standard output and standard error.
Both return the command's process id, or \-1.
.I Sysrun
runs the command and returns the first kilobyte of what it prints, with the white space at
its end removed, in a buffer that the next call reuses; it returns nil if the command prints nothing.
These three use
.IR thread (2)'s
.IR threadspawnl .
.PP
Unlike plan9port's, the library does not define
.I emalloc
and
.IR erealloc ,
nor provide
.IR estrdup :
the program provides the first two, as Plan 9's programs have their own, with the types
.B acme.h
gives:
.IP
.EX
void*	emalloc(ulong n)
void*	erealloc(void *v, ulong n)
.EE
.PP
Neither returns if the memory cannot be had, and
.I emalloc
returns its memory zeroed.
The library allocates its
.BR Win s
and
.IR winmread 's
buffers with them.
.PP
The other functions that use a window's file call
.I sysfatal
when they cannot open it, as plan9port's do;
but after
.IR winclosefiles ,
if the window's session cannot be made again, they fail, returning \-1 or nil.
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/libacme
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR acme (1),
.IR acme (4),
.IR amux (4),
.IR thread (2)
.SH BUGS
.I Openwin
takes a file descriptor where plan9port's takes a 9P client's fid, so a program that passes nil passes \-1 here.
.PP
Without
.IR amux ,
.IR winseqopen 's
lock is the program's own.
Another program that writes the window's
.B seq
between this one's write and its open takes the guard.
